%[text] # Perform Thresholding and Morphological Operations on GPU
%[text] This example shows how to perform image processing operations on a GPU. The example uses filtering to highlight the watery areas in an aerial photograph.
%[text] Read and display an image.
imOriginal = imread("concordaerial.png");
imageshow(imOriginal) %[output:3646d4cc]
%[text] Move the image to the GPU by creating a [`gpuArray`](docid:distcomp_ug#bsni4_e-1) object.
imGPUoriginal = gpuArray(imOriginal); %[output:8660cf7b]
%[text] As a preprocessing step, change the RGB image to a grayscale image. [`im2gray`](docid:matlab_ref#mw_5aab681d-ae78-41b1-afa6-ba9b49e24394) performs the conversion operation on a GPU because the input argument is a `gpuArray`.
imGPUgray = im2gray(imGPUoriginal);
%[text] View the image in the [Image Viewer](docid:images_ref#f5-471719) app and inspect the pixel values to find the value of watery areas. To use Image Viewer, you must bring the image data back onto the CPU by using the [`gather`](docid:distcomp_ug#bquolpd-1) function. As you move the mouse over the image, you can view the value of the pixel under the cursor at the bottom of the Image Viewer. In the image, areas of water are dark and have pixel values less than 70.
imageViewer(gather(imGPUgray)); %[output:0e755a36]
%[text] ![Image Viewer app window showing the aerial photograph](text:image:13e4)
%%
%[text] To get a new image that contains only the pixels with values less than 70, threshold the image on the GPU.
imWaterGPU = imGPUgray<70;
%[text] Display the thresholded image. Unlike Image Viewer, the [`imageshow`](docid:images_ref#mw_e5094e7d-fb3e-4c43-a734-2af717786811) function supports `gpuArray` input.
imageshow(imWaterGPU) %[output:29852732]
%[text] Remove small objects from the image while preserving the shape and size of larger objects by using morphological opening. The [`imopen`](docid:images_ref#f5-345703) function performs morphological opening and supports `gpuArray` input.
imWaterMask = imopen(imWaterGPU,strel("disk",5));
imageshow(imWaterMask) %[output:482e1a3c]
%%
%[text] Create a copy of the original image that will contain the enhanced data. Convert the data type to `single`.
imGPUenhanced = im2single(imGPUoriginal);
%[text] Get the blue channel from the original image. 
blueChannelOriginal = imGPUenhanced(:,:,3);
%[text] Enhance the saturation of the blue channel by increasing the strength of the blue channel for pixels where the mask is `1` (`true`).
blueChannelEnhanced = blueChannelOriginal + 0.2*single(imWaterMask);
%[text] The maximum value of the enhanced blue channel exceeds the maximum value expected of images of data type `single`. Rescale the data to the expected range \[0, 1\] by using the [`rescale`](docid:matlab_ref#mw_e944b9ed-e3a1-4137-b750-1cbb369a1d6b) function.
blueChannelEnhanced = rescale(blueChannelEnhanced);
%[text] Replace the blue channel with the enhanced blue channel.
imGPUenhanced(:,:,3) = blueChannelEnhanced;
%[text] Display the enhanced image. Pixels corresponding to water have a more saturated blue color in the enhanced image than in the original image.
imageshow(imGPUenhanced) %[output:9de2bfba]
%[text] After filtering the image on the GPU, move the data back to the CPU by using the `gather` function. Write the modified image to a file.
outCPU = gather(imGPUenhanced);
imwrite(outCPU,"concordwater.png")
